<Layout title="Morning Briefing - 07/30/26: Power Grab TX">
  <div class="post-page">
    <div class="post-content">
      <h1>⭐ Morning Briefing — Texas &amp; DFW AI Data Centers, Grid, Land Use</h1>
      <p class="briefing-subtitle">(Latest updates through July 30, 2026)</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>⚡ 1. ERCOT &amp; Grid Signals</h2>
      <p class="section-tagline">Large-Load Queue Nearly Doubles as CenterPoint Pegs 14 GW of Demand Batch-Zero Eligible</p>
      <ul>
        <li>ERCOT's large-load interconnection queue held 466,497 MW of requests as of June 2026, nearly double the 238,629 MW logged in March 2026 (ercotqueue.com tracker, July 11) — evidence the new Batch Zero process hasn't slowed the rush to file, even as qualification deadlines tightened.</li>
        <li>CenterPoint Energy officials said Tuesday (Utility Dive, July 28) that roughly 14 GW of the company's service-territory large-load requests are likely Batch Zero-eligible, and the utility now expects 50% load growth by the end of 2029, driven largely by data centers.</li>
        <li>The Texas Tribune reported Wednesday (July 29) that PUCT Chairman Thomas Gleeson sent Gov. Abbott a letter asking state lawmakers to expand ERCOT/PUCT authority over data centers ahead of the 2027 session — including direct emergency communication with large loads and mandatory registration; the story was still running via wire pickup in DFW-area outlets like the Denton Record-Chronicle this morning (July 30).</li>
        <li>A separate Denton Record-Chronicle grid dispatch published this morning (July 30) noted ERCOT is meeting record summer demand "for now," citing new renewable capacity — including a 180 MW solar farm from 174 Power Global — as part of what's kept the system stable so far this summer.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> The queue data confirms Batch Zero hasn't cooled demand — it's nearly doubled since March — which is exactly the dynamic driving PUCT's push for more legal authority over these loads.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>🏗️ 2. Data Center Construction &amp; Corporate Activity</h2>
      <p class="section-tagline">Meta and BlackRock Unveil $14 Billion, 1-Gigawatt El Paso Campus</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Meta and BlackRock announced Tuesday (July 28) a new strategic venture to build and operate a 1-gigawatt, roughly $14 billion AI data center campus in El Paso; BlackRock-managed funds will hold an 80% stake, with Meta retaining 20%, and a portion of BlackRock's investment financed through about $12.5 billion in debt (Reuters, Meta Investor Relations).</li>
        <li>The 1,000-acre campus is one of the largest single Texas data center commitments announced to date and continues Meta's pattern of using off-balance-sheet financing partners for AI infrastructure buildout.</li>
        <li>Closer to DFW, Community Impact reported Tuesday (July 29) that Westlake officials announced construction of a new data center near Keller and Southlake, to be sited south of the town's existing pump station at 13590 Denton Highway (US 377) — placing a hyperscale-adjacent project directly inside the core DFW metro rather than the exurban ring.</li>
        <li>In Young County, just west of the Wise/Parker county line, the Olney Enterprise reported this morning (July 30) that Stream Data Centers' purchase of Plug Power's 66-acre Graham site — a deal worth up to $76.5 million, first reported last week — is moving closer to finalization as "Project Saltworks," an 867-acre proposed campus.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> Meta's El Paso deal shows hyperscalers still favor West Texas for the biggest single commitments, but Westlake's in-town Keller/Southlake project is a reminder that DFW's inner suburbs, not just its exurban counties, are now squarely in play.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>🌿 3. Environmental &amp; Air-Permit Activity (TCEQ)</h2>
      <p class="section-tagline">NRG's Withdrawn Hood County Gas Plant Still Shadows New TCEQ Filings Near DFW</p>
      <ul>
        <li>NRG's withdrawal of its air permit application for the proposed 1,200-megawatt, gas-fired Tolar Power Center in Hood County (first reported in June by the Fort Worth Star-Telegram and Oil &amp; Gas Watch) remains the most recent confirmed DFW-adjacent permit action; no new Hood, Parker, Wise, Ellis, Collin, Johnson, Denton, or Tarrant county gas-plant or data-center air permit filing was confirmed as newly dated today.</li>
        <li>TCEQ's Title V current-applications tracker shows a data refresh as of July 26, 2026, but no newly posted DFW-area, data-center-linked entries were visible in today's check.</li>
        <li>Public Citizen published a "Texas Data Center Policy Guide" this week explaining the two-track system operators use statewide — New Source Review permits for new construction and Title V operating permits for major sources — context for why critics say smaller, NSR-only filings for on-site gas plants draw less public scrutiny than a single major-source Title V permit would.</li>
        <li>Statewide baseline, per Environmental Integrity Project/Texas Tribune reporting: at least 15 Texas data centers are building their own behind-the-meter gas plants, with combined emissions estimated above 130 million tons of greenhouse gases — the pollution backdrop against which any new DFW-area filing would be judged.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> No new DFW-specific TCEQ filing surfaced today — but the NSR-vs.-Title V mechanics Public Citizen just laid out are the same ones critics say let operators avoid the public notice that would otherwise have flagged a filing like NRG's now-withdrawn Tolar permit sooner.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>🏛️ 4. Land-Use &amp; Zoning Developments</h2>
      <p class="section-tagline">Westlake and Young County Show Two Faces of the Same Land Rush</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Westlake's newly announced data center near Keller/Southlake (Section 2) puts a project inside Tarrant/Denton counties' built-up core, a contrast to the exurban pattern seen in Hood, Parker, and Wise counties.</li>
        <li>In Fort Worth, the fight continues over the Zoning Commission's July 8 vote (7-4) rejecting the city's proposed data-center ordinance; five council members are now pushing for a temporary moratorium, with a City Council work session update scheduled for August 4 and a possible council vote to follow.</li>
        <li>The Young County/Graham "Project Saltworks" land deal (Section 2) — Plug Power's abandoned hydrogen site repurposed for an 867-acre data center campus — is a brownfield-to-data-center pivot, confirmed today as "moving closer to reality" in local coverage.</li>
        <li>NBC 5 DFW reported this week that opposition groups are running a statewide "week of action," including rallies, committee testimony, and webinars focused on data centers' water use and grid impact — a sign organized opposition is scaling up its tactics beyond individual county hearings.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> Fort Worth's Aug. 4 work session and the Young County deal's progress are worth tracking together — one is a city trying to slow approvals down, the other is a county-level deal moving forward regardless.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>🔌 5. Power-First Strategies &amp; On-Site Generation</h2>
      <p class="section-tagline">No New DFW-Specific Deal Today — Abilene's PowerPlay AI Shows the Model Still Spreading Statewide</p>
      <ul>
        <li>No new dedicated gas-plant, gas-plus-battery, or behind-the-meter generation announcement tied specifically to a DFW-area campus was confirmed as newly dated today.</li>
        <li>The most recent statewide example remains PowerPlay AI's plan (Pipeline and Gas Journal, July 20) for a 400-megawatt, behind-the-meter data center near Abilene backed by dedicated natural gas infrastructure — outside DFW, but the same design pattern (skip the interconnection queue, build your own gas supply) under scrutiny in the NRG Tolar withdrawal and the statewide behind-the-meter count.</li>
        <li>CenterPoint's disclosure that 14 GW of its large loads are likely Batch Zero-eligible (Section 1) is itself a signal about the alternative: operators willing to wait in ERCOT's queue rather than build on-site generation are betting the new batch process moves faster than the old study process did.</li>
        <li>Regulatory backdrop unchanged: PUCT/ERCOT's pending large-load disclosure rules (Section 1) would require big power users to report curtailment and backup-generation capability, making behind-the-meter deals more visible once finalized.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> With no new DFW power-first deal today, the open question is whether operators here follow Abilene's model (build your own gas) or CenterPoint's bet on Batch Zero — Hood County's NRG withdrawal suggests at least one DFW-area operator just chose to step back from the gas-plant route.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>📍 6. Today's Watch List</h2>
      <p class="section-tagline">Actionable Signals for Thursday, July 30</p>

      <h3>ERCOT</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Whether ERCOT's large-load queue (466,497 MW as of June) keeps growing after Batch Zero's July 24 qualification deadline</li>
        <li>Batch Zero deficiency-cure deadline: August 31</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>PUC</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Whether Gleeson's legislative asks (direct emergency contact, mandatory registration, Lone Star Act expansion) get filed as bills ahead of 2027</li>
        <li>Confirmation of which specific CenterPoint large loads qualify under its 14 GW Batch Zero estimate</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>TCEQ</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Any newly posted DFW-area (Hood, Parker, Wise, Ellis, Collin, Johnson, Denton, Tarrant) Title V or NSR filing tied to a data center or gas plant</li>
        <li>Whether NRG refiles a revised, smaller Tolar Power Center permit in Hood County</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>County Zoning (DFW + Surrounding Counties)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Fort Worth City Council's August 4 work session update on the data-center moratorium process</li>
        <li>Westlake's formal site-plan and permitting steps for its Keller/Southlake-area data center</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Corporate Filings</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Stream Data Centers' "Project Saltworks" Young County site plan and any remaining approvals</li>
        <li>Meta/BlackRock El Paso venture — financing close and any Texas-specific incentive filings</li>
      </ul>

